From f90b2b76e50d02072bceb093720dddd450376a6c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 20:29:02 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 6/7] feat(algorithm): StockhamFFTSplit; FFT routes from tables
 measured on two hosts
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

`StockhamFFTSplit` runs the same Stockham passes on blocks of W real parts followed by W imaginary parts, so the butterflies' twiddle products need no re/im lane swaps; the first pass de-interleaves, the last re-interleaves.
It is the fastest kernel for large N at 128- and 256-bit vectors on both measured hosts, and `FFT` now routes there.

The route tables now hold, per type, vector width and N, the candidate whose slowest host is fastest, merged from `bm_fft` runs on an Intel Xeon @ 2.1 GHz (AVX-512 VM, GCC 15) and an AMD Ryzen AI 9 HX 370 (GCC 16), each at -march=x86-64, x86-64-v3 and native.
Where StockhamFFT is slower than SimdFFT on either host (double, 128 bit, 2^12 and 2^14 .. 2^18 on the HX 370), the table keeps SimdFFT.

Stream-mode time of the routed kernel relative to `SimdFFT` on the slower host, range over N = 2^6 .. 2^20:

| vector width | float       | double      |
| ------------ | ----------: | ----------: |
| 128 bit      | 0.39 – 0.83 | 0.71 – 1.00 |
| 256 bit      | 0.45 – 0.71 | 0.62 – 0.96 |
| 512 bit      | 0.37 – 0.74 | 0.63 – 0.99 |

### Changes
- `StockhamFFTSplit.hpp`: kernel, default plan and predicate, C++23 and C++26 backends
- `FFTRoutes.hpp`: `FFTKernel::stockhamSplit`; merged tables with the hosts they come from
- `fft.hpp`: `FFT::stockhamFFTSplit` and its dispatch
- `qa_StockhamFFTSplit` suite in `qa_StockhamFFT.cpp`: long-double ground truth 2^6–2^16 (2^20 double), impulse, in-place; routing checks every kernel
